/micalpixel/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/micalpixel/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/micalpixel/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/micalpixel/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/micalpixel/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/micalpixel/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/micalpixel/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/micalpixel/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-02-11T17:32:02Z","updated_at":"2026-02-11T17:43:54Z","body":"I have noticed this behavior as well.\n\nViewport width/height is 1280 x 720 in project settings.\n\nBut on the first \"Run Project\" after launching Godot, it uses a viewport of 1237 x 696.\n\nGodot v4.6.stable - Windows 11 (build 26200) - Multi-window, 2 monitors - Direct3D 12 (Forward+) - dedicated AMD Radeon RX 9060 XT (Advanced Micro Devices, Inc.; 32.0.22042.1) - AMD Ryzen 7 9800X3D 8-Core Processor (8 threads) - 61.55 GiB memory\n\n\nEDIT: I was able to reproduce this very easily - by launching Godot, then creating a new project, setting and saving a Node2D scene for the root, checking the viewport settings, and running it.\n\n<img width=\"1190\" height=\"288\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2bb88f65-3800-4a66-9931-3587a30698e0\" />\n\nIt will be incorrect on the first run after launching Godot. But on the second run and afterwards, the dimensions will match the project settings.\n<img width=\"1913\" height=\"1157\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c27f2bb9-3db5-47dc-bd82-e37ae2162bc3\" />","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/3885890638/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null}]